From ufb5badd8a04823fb at groups.google.com  Wed Jul 29 11:26:04 2015
From: ufb5badd8a04823fb at groups.google.com (Ralf Mullers)
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2015 11:26:04 +0000
Subject: How to analyse repeated counts of the same transect?
Message-ID: <HBfqfK9jN4k.xhfpxxpiCQAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/HBfqfK9jN4k/m/xhfpxxpiCQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

We want to estimate game numbers in our reserve. The reserve is 1100 ha and the transect we drove is in total 13 km (a road count). Because visibility is limited due to dense vegetation cover, we repeated our drive counts 8 times. Is it possible to incorporate these repeat counts into Distance and how do we do this? Also, is it possible to include several species, or should we analyse each species separately?

In short; one region (the reserve), one line transect (13 km round trip) and 8 repeated counts on that line transect.

How do I structure the data in Distance to analyse this? Do I need to break the transect line in smaller parts for example?

Thanks, Ralf

From uf2122b34f1aa6e95 at groups.google.com  Wed Jul 29 12:51:25 2015
From: uf2122b34f1aa6e95 at groups.google.com (Eric Rexstad)
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2015 12:51:25 +0000
Subject: Re: [distance-sampling] How to analyse repeated counts of the same transect?
Message-ID: <HBfqfK9jN4k.qEFnIgrRBgAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <HBfqfK9jN4k.xhfpxxpiCQAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
References: <HBfqfK9jN4k.xhfpxxpiCQAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/HBfqfK9jN4k/m/qEFnIgrRBgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Ralf:

This survey design is not great for several reasons:
- roads do not sample the study area in a random or representative manner,
- animals know where roads are situated and are either attracted or repulsed by roads, leading to non-uniform distribution of animals at all perpendicular distances from the roads, and
- you only have a single replicate transect; this defeats your ability to measure encounter rate variability between transects; this is a fundamental element in assessing your ability to extrapolate from the places you sampled to the places you did not sample.

Breaking the transect into smaller parts does not solve issues 1 and 2 and does not effectively resolve issue 3.

Each of those difficulties makes it challenging for you to make legitimate inferences about the number of game animals in your reserve.

The direct answer to your questions is the amount of effort associated with your (single) transect is the length of the transect multiplied by the number of visits; in your case 13x8=104km.  You should entertain the possibility that different species have different detection functions, so you should enter all species into the same Distance project and either analyse them separately, or treat species as a factor covariate or strata in modelling your detection function.   That way you can statistically assess whether species share or have distinct detection functions.  Consult

Marques, T.A., Thomas, L., Fancy, S.G. and Buckland, S.T. 2007.  Improving estimates of bird density using multiple covariate distance sampling.  The Auk 124, 1229-1243.

